Conclusions: I. All cats are birds. II. Some birds are dogs.', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Syllogism', 'Both I and II follow', 'I follows by chaining ''All cats are dogs'' and ''All dogs are birds''. II follows because ''All dogs are birds'' implies that some birds are dogs.'),
(142, 2, NULL, 'Syllogism - Some and All', 'Statements: Some pens are books. All books are tables.
Conclusions: I. Some pens are tables. II. All tables are books.', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Syllogism', 'Only I follows', 'The pens that are books are also tables, so I follows. II reverses ''All books are tables'', which is not allowed.'),
(143, 2, NULL, 'Syllogism - Negative Statement', 'Statements: No flower is a tree. Some trees are plants.
Conclusions: I. Some plants are not flowers. II. No plant is a flower.', 'HARD', 'MCQ', 'Syllogism', 'Only I follows', 'The plants that are trees cannot be flowers (no flower is a tree), so some plants are not flowers. II is too strong: other plants may be flowers.'),
(144, 2, NULL, 'Blood Relations - Father''s Brother''s Son', 'Pointing to a photograph, Riya says, ''He is the son of my father''s only brother.'' How is the man related to Riya?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Blood Relations', 'Cousin', 'My father''s brother is my uncle, and his son is my cousin.'),
(145, 2, NULL, 'Blood Relations - Generation Chain', 'A is B''s sister. C is B''s mother. D is C''s father. How is A related to D?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Blood Relations', 'Granddaughter', 'C is the mother of both A and B, and D is C''s father. So A is D''s daughter''s daughter, i.e. his granddaughter.'),
(146, 2, NULL, 'Blood Relations - Only Daughter of My Mother', 'Introducing a man, a woman says, ''His mother is the only daughter of my mother.'' How is the woman related to the man?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Blood Relations', 'Mother', 'The only daughter of the woman''s mother is the woman herself, so she is the man''s mother.'),
(147, 2, NULL, 'Direction Sense - Return Path', 'A man walks 5 km north, turns right and walks 3 km, then turns right again and walks 5 km. How far, and in which direction, is he from his starting point?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Direction Sense', '3 km East', 'North 5 km, then east 3 km, then south 5 km brings him back to the same latitude, 3 km east of the start.'),
(148, 2, NULL, 'Direction Sense - Turning Angles', 'Rohan faces East. He turns 90 degrees clockwise and then 180 degrees anticlockwise. Which direction is he facing now?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Direction Sense', 'North', 'East turned 90 degrees clockwise is South. Turning 180 degrees from South gives North.'),
(149, 2, NULL, 'Direction Sense - Four Legged Walk', 'Aman starts at P and walks 10 m west. He turns left and walks 10 m, then turns right and walks 10 m, then turns right again and walks 10 m. Where is he with respect to P?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Direction Sense', '20 m West', 'West 10 m, left (south) 10 m, right (west) 10 m, right (north) 10 m. Net: 20 m west and 0 m north-south.'),
(150, 2, NULL, 'Coding-Decoding - Letter Shift', 'In a certain code, ROAD is written as URDG. How is SWIM written in that code?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Coding-Decoding', 'VZLP', 'Every letter is moved 3 places forward (R->U, O->R, A->D, D->G). So S->V, W->Z, I->L, M->P gives VZLP.'),
(151, 2, NULL, 'Coding-Decoding - Next Letter', 'If MONDAY is coded as NPOEBZ, how is FRIDAY coded?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Coding-Decoding', 'GSJEBZ', 'Each letter is replaced by the next letter of the alphabet: F->G, R->S, I->J, D->E, A->B, Y->Z.'),
(152, 2, NULL, 'Coding-Decoding - Reverse Order', 'If TABLE is coded as ELBAT, how is CHAIR coded?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Coding-Decoding', 'RIAHC', 'The word is written backwards: TABLE -> ELBAT, so CHAIR -> RIAHC.'),
(153, 2, NULL, 'Ranking - Position from the Other End', 'In a row of 40 students, Ravi is 12th from the left. What is his position from the right?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Ranking', '29th', 'Position from the right = total - position from the left + 1 = 40 - 12 + 1 = 29.'),
(154, 2, NULL, 'Seating - Five Friends in a Row', 'Five friends A, B, C, D and E sit in a row facing north. A is to the left of B but to the right of C. D is to the right of B but to the left of E. Who sits in the middle?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Seating Arrangement', 'B', 'C is left of A, A is left of B, B is left of D, D is left of E. The order is C, A, B, D, E, so B is in the middle.'),
(155, 2, NULL, 'Ranking - Total Students from Two Ranks', 'In a class, Priya ranks 7th from the top and 28th from the bottom. How many students are in the class?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Ranking', '34', 'Total = rank from top + rank from bottom - 1 = 7 + 28 - 1 = 34.'),
(156, 2, NULL, 'Analogy - Place of Work', 'Doctor : Hospital :: Teacher : ?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Analogy', 'School', 'A doctor works in a hospital in the same way a teacher works in a school.'),
(157, 2, NULL, 'Odd One Out - Planets', 'Which one is different from the others: Mercury, Venus, Moon, Mars?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Odd One Out', 'Moon', 'Mercury, Venus and Mars are planets. The Moon is a natural satellite of the Earth.'),
(158, 2, NULL, 'Synonym - Abundant', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to ABUNDANT.', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Synonyms', 'Plentiful', 'Abundant means existing in large quantities, which is the same as plentiful.');


Which gas is released during photosynthesis?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Reading Comprehension', 'Oxygen', 'The passage states that oxygen is released as a by-product.'),
(165, 7, NULL, 'Encapsulation - Hiding Internal State', 'Which OOP principle bundles data with the methods that operate on it and restricts direct access to the internal state?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Encapsulation', 'Encapsulation', 'Encapsulation keeps fields private and exposes controlled access through methods.'),
(166, 7, NULL, 'Compile-Time Polymorphism', 'Which of the following is an example of compile-time (static) polymorphism?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Polymorphism', 'Method overloading', 'Overloaded methods are chosen by the compiler from the argument types. Overriding is resolved at run time.'),
(167, 7, NULL, 'Abstract Class vs Interface', 'Which statement about an abstract class and an interface in Java is TRUE?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Abstraction', 'An abstract class can have constructors and instance fields, an interface cannot have instance fields', 'Abstract classes can hold state and constructors. Interfaces only declare constants (static final fields), and a class can extend only one class.'),
(168, 7, NULL, 'Concrete Class and Abstract Methods', 'A non-abstract class extends an abstract class but does not implement one of its abstract methods. What happens?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Abstraction', 'Compilation error', 'A concrete class must implement every inherited abstract method or be declared abstract itself.'),
(169, 7, NULL, 'Constructors and Inheritance', 'In Java, are constructors inherited by subclasses?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Inheritance', 'No, a subclass must declare its own constructors', 'Constructors are not members that are inherited. A subclass constructor calls a superclass constructor with super(...).'),
(170, 7, NULL, 'Implicit super() Call', 'A superclass declares only a constructor that takes an int parameter. A subclass constructor does not call super(...) explicitly. What happens?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Inheritance', 'Compilation error, because there is no no-argument superclass constructor to call implicitly', 'The compiler inserts an implicit super() call. If the superclass has no accessible no-argument constructor, the subclass must call a constructor explicitly.'),
(171, 7, NULL, 'Multiple Inheritance and the Diamond Problem', 'How does Java avoid the diamond problem that arises with multiple inheritance?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Inheritance', 'A class can extend only one class, but may implement many interfaces', 'Single class inheritance removes ambiguity. For interfaces with conflicting default methods, the class must override the method.'),
(172, 7, NULL, 'Virtual Functions in C++', 'What is the main purpose of virtual functions in C++?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Polymorphism', 'To enable run-time polymorphism through the base class pointer or reference', 'A virtual call is dispatched on the actual object type (via the vtable), not the pointer type.'),
(173, 7, NULL, 'Static Methods and Instance Members', 'Which statement about a static method in Java is correct?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Static Members', 'It cannot directly access non-static instance fields', 'A static method belongs to the class, so it has no this reference and cannot use instance members without an object.'),
(174, 7, NULL, 'Composition - Has-A Relationship', 'A Car class contains an Engine object. Which relationship does this describe?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Class Relationships', 'Composition (has-a)', 'Using another object as a field is composition. Inheritance describes is-a relationships.'),
(175, 7, NULL, 'SOLID - Single Responsibility Principle', 'What does the Single Responsibility Principle state?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'SOLID', 'A class should have only one reason to change', 'Each class should focus on one responsibility so changes to one concern do not ripple into others.'),
(176, 7, NULL, 'SOLID - Liskov Substitution Principle', 'What does the Liskov Substitution Principle require?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'SOLID', 'Objects of a subclass must be usable wherever the superclass is expected without breaking correctness', 'Subtypes must honour the contract of the base type. A Square that breaks the behaviour of Rectangle''s setters is a classic violation.'),
(177, 7, NULL, 'SOLID - Open/Closed Principle', 'What does the Open/Closed Principle say?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'SOLID', 'Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification', 'You should be able to add new behaviour (for example, a new subclass) without editing existing, tested code.'),
(178, 7, NULL, 'SOLID - Dependency Inversion Principle', 'What does the Dependency Inversion Principle recommend?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'SOLID', 'High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not on concrete implementations', 'Depending on interfaces lets you swap implementations (for example, a database) without changing the business logic.'),
(179, 7, NULL, 'SOLID - Interface Segregation Principle', 'What does the Interface Segregation Principle state?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'SOLID', 'Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use', 'Prefer several small, focused interfaces over one large one.'),
(180, 7, NULL, 'Singleton Pattern', 'What does the Singleton design pattern guarantee?', 'EASY', 'MCQ', 'Design Patterns', 'Only one instance of the class exists and it has a global access point', 'Singleton restricts instantiation to one object and provides access to it.'),
(181, 7, NULL, 'Factory Pattern', 'What is the main purpose of the Factory pattern?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Design Patterns', 'To create objects without exposing the instantiation logic to the client', 'A factory method returns objects of a common type, so client code does not depend on concrete classes.'),
(182, 7, NULL, 'Observer Pattern', 'Which design pattern defines a one-to-many dependency so that when one object changes state all its dependents are notified?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Design Patterns', 'Observer', 'A subject keeps a list of observers and notifies them of changes, as in event listeners.'),
(183, 7, NULL, 'Strategy Pattern', 'Which pattern encapsulates a family of interchangeable algorithms so the algorithm can be chosen at run time?', 'MEDIUM', 'MCQ', 'Design Patterns', 'Strategy', 'Strategy moves each algorithm into its own class behind a common interface (for example, different sorting or payment strategies).');


